  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Mainly dry with seasonable temperatures through the \nweekend. \n\nA few showers and embedded weak storms are possible over our \ncoastal waters early this morning as the cold front pushes \noffshore. By sunrise, the front should be fully through pushing \nout any remaining showers. Weak mid-level troughing will persist\nwith drier and cooler conditions in the post-frontal airmass. \n925 mb temperatures drop a few degrees from yesterday yielding \ncooler high temperatures in the upper 70s to near 80 for \nsouthern New England today. The airmass will be noticeable drier\nas well bringing more comfortable humidity levels as surface \nhigh pressure builds in from the west. This will also keep winds\non the lighter side with light NW winds and local sea breezes \nfor the coastal areas.\n\nSurface high pressure builds further over the region Friday \ncontinuing through the weekend with flow aloft becoming more \nzonal. Weak subsident flow will keep conditions dry. Winds \nremain light under surface high pressure with winds becoming \neasterly for the weekend. 925mb temperatures range 16-21C \nsupporting highs in the low to mid 80s in most spots, perhaps a \ntad cooler in the immediate coastal areas where sea \nbreezes/onshore flow will be prevalent. Surface high pressure \npushes offshore through the day Sunday as a mid-level trough \napproaches from the west. The drier airmass should stay in place\nduring the day suppressing most shower development. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Unsettled weather for early to mid-week next week \nwith periodic chances for scattered showers and thunderstorms.\n \nThe pattern becomes more unsettled early next week with a mid-level \ntrough positioned across the northeast and a series of disturbances \nmoving through the flow. Overall there is a good signal for \nincreasing moisture with PWATs slightly above normal which will \nsupport increased chances for periodic scattered showers and \nthunderstorms early next week through mid-week. Temperatures \nlean toward seasonable values in the low 80s.",
